## Why This Is Asked
This question tests dynamic plugin architecture, streaming parsing, and runtime extensibility without recompiling the core pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Plugin registry pattern using Factory/Strategy design
- Streaming I/O with backpressure-aware adapters
- Runtime extensibility and loose coupling for testability

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Parser {
  parse(input: AsyncIterable<Uint8Array>): AsyncIterable<Record<string, unknown>>;
}

type ParserFactory = () => Parser;

class Registry {
  private factories = new Map<string, ParserFactory>();

  register(extension: string, factory: ParserFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(extension.toLowerCase(), factory);
  }

  getParser(extension: string): Parser | undefined {
    return this.factories.get(extension.toLowerCase())?.();
  }
}

// Usage example
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('xml', () => new XmlParser());
registry.register('yaml', () => new YamlParser());
registry.register('avro', () => new AvroParser());

async function processFile(path: string, data: AsyncIterable<Uint8Array>) {
  const ext = path.split('.').pop();
  const parser = registry.getParser(ext);
  if (!parser) throw new Error(`Unsupported format: ${ext}`);

  for await (const record of parser.parse(data)) {
    // Process record with backpressure handling
    await processRecord(record);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design runtime extensibility for security-critical components, including hot-reload and safe concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime pluggability via a policy registry
- Evaluation order and conflict resolution
- Thread-safety and atomic swaps for hot-reload
- RBAC/ABAC/PBAC composition and performance trade-offs

## Code Example
```java
interface Policy {
  boolean evaluate(User user, Resource resource, Action action);
  String name();
}
class PolicyRegistry {
  private final java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference<java.util.Map<String, Policy>> policies =
      new java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference<>(java.util.Collections.emptyMap());

  public void register(String name, Policy p) {
    var next = new java.util.HashMap<>(policies.get());
    next.put(name, p);
    policies.set(java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap(next));
  }

  public boolean evaluate(User user, Resource resource, Action action) {
    for (Policy p : policies.get().values()) {
      if (p.evaluate(user, resource, action)) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  public void reload(java.util.Map<String, Policy> newPolicies) {
    policies.set(java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap(new java.util.HashMap<>(newPolicies)));
  }
}
```

